To draw a chessboard from a FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) string in LaTeX, the most powerful and standard package to use is **`chessboard`** (which works in tandem with the **`skak`** or **`xskak`** package). 

The `chessboard` package has a built-in command, `\chessboard[fen=...]`, which automatically parses the FEN string and renders the board.

Here is a complete, ready-to-compile LaTeX document showing how to do this:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{geometry}
\geometry{margin=1in}

% Load the chessboard package, which automatically loads xskak
\usepackage{chessboard}

\title{Rendering Chess FEN in \LaTeX}
\author{Chess Assistant}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Example Chessboard}

Below is a chessboard rendered directly from a FEN string. The FEN used here represents the starting position of a standard game:
\begin{center}
    \texttt{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1}
\end{center}

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    % Use the \chessboard command and pass the FEN string to the "fen" key
    \chessboard[fen=rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1]
    \caption{The starting position rendered using LaTeX.}
\end{figure}

\section{Customized Board (Mid-Game Position)}

You can also customize the look of the board (e.g., adding coordinates, changing colors, or highlighting squares). Here is a mid-game position:

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \chessboard[
        % The FEN string for the position
        fen=r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3,
        % Customize the size of the board
        maxfield=h8,
        % Show coordinates on the borders
        showmover=true,
        % Highlight the last move (e.g., Bishop to b5)
        color=red!50,
        colorbackfields={b5}
    ]
    \caption{Ruy Lopez opening position with the White Bishop highlighted.}
\end{figure}

\end{document}
```

### How to Compile This Code:
1. Copy the code above.
2. Paste it into any LaTeX editor (such as Overleaf, TeXworks, or Texmaker).
3. Make sure your LaTeX distribution is up to date (the `chessboard` package is included in standard distributions like TeX Live, MiKTeX, and MacTeX).
4. Compile using **pdfLaTeX** or **XeLaTeX**.

### Key Features of this Approach:
* **Automatic Parsing:** You do not need to manually place each piece. The `\chessboard[fen=...]` command reads the FEN string and places the correct black and white pieces automatically.
* **Customization:** The package allows you to easily change the size of the board, add labels, highlight specific squares (like the red highlight on square `b5` in the second example), or even show only a portion of the board.

